Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.120
Phenotyping algorithm is very important in&nbsp;
supporting genome-wide association study.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:18.000
What is a genome-wide association study? It's an&nbsp;
approach that involves scanning biomarkers such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.640
as single nucleotide polymorphism,&nbsp;
or SNPs, from DNA of many people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.760
in order to find genetic variation, associated&nbsp;
with a particular disease field phenotypes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:32.880
Once new genetic associations are&nbsp;
identified, researchers can use that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:38.080
information to develop better strategies.&nbsp;
To detect and treat and prevent diseases.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:42.400
So, how are genomic&nbsp;
wide-association studies conducted?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:49.280
To run a genomic wide-association study, or&nbsp;
GWAS, we first identify the disease phenotypes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.240
Then group the participants into these&nbsp;
two groups, cases and these are people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:00.160
with disease phenotypes. And controls, those are&nbsp;
similar patient without the disease phenotype.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:06.720
Then we need to obtain DNA samples from&nbsp;
all these participants. From DNA samples,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.840
we can use lab machines to quickly survey each&nbsp;
participant's genomes for genetic variation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:18.880
Which are called single-nucleotide polymorphism&nbsp;
or SNP. If certain genetic variation have found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.440
to be significantly more frequent in the people&nbsp;
with the disease phenotypes compared to people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.880
without the disease phenotype. These variations&nbsp;
are said to be associated with the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:35.680
We do this by computing the frequencies of SNPs&nbsp;
on the cases and on the controls. Then based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:42.160
the frequency, we calculate the odds ratio. From&nbsp;
there, we can calculate the corresponding p-value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:48.160
for the odds ratio. If the p-value is small,&nbsp;
then we conclude this variation is significant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.800
The associated genetic variations&nbsp;
can serve as powerful pointers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.440
the region of the human genome&nbsp;
that may cause the disease.

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:02.400
Here's an example showing more details on how&nbsp;
the GWAS is computed. We first identified the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.920
cases and controls. That is, the people with&nbsp;
the disease phenotype and the people without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.840
the disease phenotype. In this case, we have&nbsp;
4000 patients with the disease phenotype and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:19.840
6000 patients without the disease phenotype.&nbsp;
Then we iterate over all the SNPs to compare the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:27.840
relevant frequencies. For instance, for SNP1 for&nbsp;
the control group, we have 2676 out of 6000 has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:34.000
the corresponding variation G, at this location.&nbsp;
And the frequency of G in this case is 44.6%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:42.880
In the case group, we have 2104 out of 4000 with&nbsp;
the corresponding variation G at this location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:50.720
So, the frequency is 52.6%. If we go through&nbsp;
the calculation, we'll find now the P-value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:57.920
is 5 times 10 to the minus 15. Which means,&nbsp;
this is extremely significant. We can conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.840
the same calculation on SNP2 and find out the&nbsp;
P-value here is 0.33 which is not significant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:10.000
To support GWAS study, we need to know high&nbsp;
quality phenotypes on the cases and controls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.040
in order to perform this calculation, that's&nbsp;
why phenotyping algorithm, is very important.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.680
As we have shown in the&nbsp;
genome-wide association studies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.520
we need phenotypic data in order to analyze&nbsp;
genomic data. But in general, why do we care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:32.240
about phenotyping algorithms in genomic study? In&nbsp;
fact, many people argue that we need rich and deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:38.880
phenotypic data in order to analyze genomic data.&nbsp;
Especially as sequencing technology improves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:44.160
the cost of generating genomic data is&nbsp;
dropping so fast over time. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.040
cost of computing or Moore's Law cannot keep up&nbsp;
with the improvement of sequencing technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.800
which means we'll have more and more genomic data&nbsp;
in near future about many individuals. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:59.440
due to the complexity of the electronic health&nbsp;
record, the cost for generating high quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.720
phenotypic data is actually increasing, while&nbsp;
the cost of genomic data is dropping drastically.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.320
That is why we really need to invent better&nbsp;
phenotyping algorithms to reduce the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:21.680
of acquiring high quality phenotypic&nbsp;
data and to support genomic studies.

